WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.859 --> 00:00:10.389
Since the end of World War Two, the world
has been built on an idea called deterrence.

00:00:10.389 --> 00:00:15.969
It says if you attack me or my allies, I'll
retaliate, so we're both better off not fighting.

00:00:15.969 --> 00:00:24.219
It's why the Cold War stayed cold, and why
there hasn't been a war between major powers

00:00:24.219 --> 00:00:25.840
in 70 years.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:27.669
It's kept the world stable.

00:00:27.669 --> 00:00:31.529
It's the reason we still have nuclear weapons
—&nbsp;and the reason we haven't used them since

00:00:31.529 --> 00:00:33.300
1945.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.180
But deterrence is changing.

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:40.030
New forms of warfare are blurring the old
lines and rewriting the old rules.So I went

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:46.530
to ask Secretary of Defense Ash Carter what
this means for the world and for the US.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:50.660
He said the stakes are high, and that the
military's challenge will be to establish

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:59.460
new norms of deterrence to convince America's
adversaries that the old rules still hold.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.920
It has the word "terror" inside it, right?

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:02.920
"Deterrence."

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:07.150
That's what it's about, is you scare someone
away from doing something you don't want them

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:11.140
to do by making them fear the consequences.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:16.450
To do that, they must know what the consequences
are, and you're right, it needs to be understood.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:23.080
There needs to be some sort of normality to
the idea that this threat will be offered

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:24.420
and it should deter.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:31.800
As we get into new domains, like cyber and
space, and we try to situate old and very

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:37.400
solid ideas like deterrence in those new domains,
it requires some thought.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:43.220
The old rules of deterrence set hard, clear
lines for what would trigger a military retaliation,

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:46.299
and those lines usually fall across b orders.

00:01:46.299 --> 00:01:49.960
But no one knows how or where to draw that
sort of line when it comes to things like

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:52.030
cyber attacks.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:56.380
In the physical world, it's easy to tell when
you're under attack by a foreign military,

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:57.460
but not so online.

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:03.509
In 2013, for example, hackers based in Iran
accessed the control system for a small dam

00:02:03.509 --> 00:02:08.289
in New York state, but it took months for
US investigators to accuse the Iranian government

00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:09.420
of being behind it.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:14.099
The hackers didn't cause any physical damage,
but they could have, and it raised questions

00:02:14.099 --> 00:02:19.090
— which are still unanswered — about when
a hack becomes like a military attack, and

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:21.590
when and how the US is obligated to respond.

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:24.300
If you attack us in cyber space, it's an attack.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:25.780
I'm very straightforward.

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:28.720
An attack is an attack.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:35.920
We won't necessarily respond in space, or
respond in cyber, we may respond in some other

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:42.370
way, but we will respond and you need to understand
that: that if you're attacking American interests,

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:45.230
there will be a response.

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:49.620
But it's important to communicate that fact,
that you're prepared for it, we know exactly

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:55.760
what we're going to do, and you will regret
the consequences of your act and you should

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:00.879
be fearful of those consequences and thus
not do it in the first place.

00:03:00.879 --> 00:03:05.060
The threat isn't just that new tools of warfare
like cyber attacks let countries get away

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:07.190
with things they normally couldn't.

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:10.950
In regular war, we have what are called norms
of proportionality.

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:12.920
You bomb me, I bomb you back.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:16.390
But tools like cyber are so new, there are
no norms yet.

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:20.970
No one knows what sort of response to expect,
and Carter says this can be dangerous.

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:25.781
Proportionality does mean if you do something,
I'm going to do something back to you, but

00:03:25.781 --> 00:03:30.819
I'm not necessarily going to do something
that's out of proportion and that makes it

00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:34.430
necessary for you then to do more, and more,
and more.

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:36.880
That's called escalation, obviously nobody
wants that.

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:42.180
The US, he says, has to have responses strong
enough to make any adversary stand down — but

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:46.580
not so strong that it could make the other
country feel obligated to respond, which is

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:48.450
how things could spiral out of control.

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:54.120
So whenever we think about deterrence responses,
we craft them in such a way that they are

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:59.620
not by themselves intentionally or unintentionally
escalatory.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:05.050
We always have retained the capability to
escalate still further, that's part of deterring

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:06.660
the next move.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:09.860
But it's not our intention, in general, to
escalate something.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:15.920
It's to stop it from ever occurring in the
first place, and if it does occur, to punish

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:20.030
it quickly, which makes the opponent say,
"Okay, I realize my mistake.

00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:23.379
I stand down from my objectives," and then
it's the end of it.

00:04:23.379 --> 00:04:25.159
But it's not just cyber war.

00:04:25.159 --> 00:04:29.909
Russia is developing new postmodern techniques
known as hybrid war.

00:04:29.909 --> 00:04:32.789
Special forces dressed up as vigilantes.

00:04:32.789 --> 00:04:35.539
Attacks meant to create chaos more than seize
territory.

00:04:35.539 --> 00:04:40.939
Propaganda and misinformation meant to spark
panic or even popular unrest.

00:04:40.939 --> 00:04:46.300
Deterrence works on certainty —&nbsp;who is
doing what, and what actions will spark retaliation.

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:48.979
But hybrid war is about creating uncertainty.

00:04:48.979 --> 00:04:53.629
It makes it harder to determine who's doing
what, and blurs the lines between what is

00:04:53.629 --> 00:04:54.759
and isn't war.

00:04:54.759 --> 00:04:59.720
And, like with cyber war, the rules of hybrid
war are still unclear.

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:03.880
Carter says the way to deal with that is by
keeping lines of communication open so that

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:09.919
both side can control any misunderstanding
or escalation before it gets out of control.

00:05:09.919 --> 00:05:25.740
It's very important to keep dialogue, even
with potential enemies.

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:30.969
That's why I believe, among many other things,
very strongly in military-to-military dialogue

00:05:30.969 --> 00:05:35.860
and dialogue between me and my counterparts.

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:44.460
You can't stop a conflict that your enemy
deliberately provokes, but you can try to

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:48.650
prevent ones that they blunder into by underestimating
you.

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:52.041
One of the ways you do that is by signaling
clearly and having dialogue.

00:05:52.041 --> 00:05:56.839
Are you satisfied with the level of military-to-military
communication with Russia and China?

00:05:56.839 --> 00:06:04.289
No, I'd like to see more in general, but it
takes two to tango.

00:06:04.289 --> 00:06:12.080
It's not just the amount, it's the character
of the dialogue and the willingness of those

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:18.060
parties to have a dialogue of a kind that
we would regard as fruitful.

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:19.800
That's not everything that it should be.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:24.909
In the last year of the Obama administration,
Carter has been arguing that the American

00:06:24.909 --> 00:06:29.849
military needs to prepare for a new era of
great power competition, primarily with Russia

00:06:29.849 --> 00:06:31.419
and with China.

00:06:31.419 --> 00:06:34.630
That means an era where deterrence is even
more important.

00:06:34.630 --> 00:06:38.669
And that deterrence, ultimately, is backed
up by nuclear weapons.

00:06:38.669 --> 00:06:47.550
My bedrock of this department here is our
commitment to nuclear security, and also to

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:49.349
deterrence in our nuclear arsenal.

00:06:49.349 --> 00:06:56.629
It's not in the headlines every day, and thank
goodness for that, but it's in the back of

00:06:56.629 --> 00:06:59.279
my mind every day.

00:06:59.279 --> 00:07:04.619
The US has about 7,100 nuclear weapons, of
which about 1,500 are deployed.

00:07:04.619 --> 00:07:06.629
Russia has about the same.

00:07:06.629 --> 00:07:09.520
Combined, that's 90% of all nuclear weapons.

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:14.530
They mainly exist to never to be used —&nbsp;to
deter other nuclear states.

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:19.189
But there's a strange logic to this: to make
sure we never have to use them, we need to

00:07:19.189 --> 00:07:24.199
credibly threaten to use weapons that would
kill millions and send the world into nuclear

00:07:24.199 --> 00:07:25.199
winter.

00:07:25.199 --> 00:07:29.639
To ask you about that deterrence, there's
a story that I love from when Dick Cheney

00:07:29.639 --> 00:07:30.699
had your job.

00:07:30.699 --> 00:07:36.309
In 1989, he was getting a briefing on US nuclear
war plans, and there was a slide that came

00:07:36.309 --> 00:07:39.610
up for retaliatory strike plans for Moscow.

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:44.089
One of the things it showed was, I think it
was something like 70 strikes on one radar

00:07:44.089 --> 00:07:46.749
facility in some suburb of Moscow.

00:07:46.749 --> 00:07:50.379
He turned to his aides and he said, "What
the hell is this?

00:07:50.379 --> 00:07:52.709
Why are we doing this kind of thing?"

00:07:52.709 --> 00:07:56.669
The reason I bring that up is I think that's
the reaction a lot of people have when they

00:07:56.669 --> 00:08:01.529
are confronted with the logic of nuclear deterrence,
and they see its ends.

00:08:01.529 --> 00:08:05.229
What do you tell people when they have that
reaction?

00:08:05.229 --> 00:08:08.309
&nbsp;I do, I hear it all the time.

00:08:08.309 --> 00:08:15.330
You never get quite used to how terrible such
a situation would be.

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:23.739
But until someone has an alternative to deterrence
as dealing with someone who might use nuclear

00:08:23.739 --> 00:08:30.239
weapons against you — and we haven't found,
in all those years since the Manhattan Project,

00:08:30.239 --> 00:08:38.399
any effective defense against nuclear weapons
— until those are found, the only defense

00:08:38.399 --> 00:08:41.560
we have is the threat of retaliation.

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:45.670
As the lines of deterrence blur, what does
that mean for nuclear weapons?

00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:50.700
The US plans to spend $1 trillion on modernizing
its nuclear weapons, and Russia is making

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:52.560
upgrades as well.

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:56.300
Carter says this is about maintaining the
status quo, a balance between the nuclear

00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:58.740
powers that keeps them away from conflict.

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:01.720
But he acknowledges that this carries some
risk.

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:03.061
Do you ever want to use it?

00:09:03.061 --> 00:09:04.061
Of course not.

00:09:04.061 --> 00:09:07.560
That's the whole purpose, is never to use
it.

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:15.430
Thank goodness in all these decades since
1945, a nuclear weapon has not been used in

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:16.670
anger since then.

00:09:16.670 --> 00:09:19.230
That's a remarkable achievement.

00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:22.240
But it's one I don't take for granted.

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:27.160
But with the growing uncertainty about what
does and doesn't triggers retaliation, and

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:31.879
about what kind of retaliation to expect,
I asked Carter whether he worried that an

00:09:31.879 --> 00:09:35.440
unintended escalation to war could happen.

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:40.959
He said the answer was for the US military
to replace that uncertainty with as much certainty

00:09:40.959 --> 00:09:42.500
as it could bring.

00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:45.690
We have awesome power in our military.

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:53.329
We're not eager to use it, and we hope it
causes others not to do provocative things,

00:09:53.329 --> 00:09:57.810
but if they do, I don't have any doubt that
we will prevail.

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:02.430
We certainly think through every scenario
that is possible and make sure that we have

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:10.189
plans that provide for the most sensible thing
we could do at the time with the capabilities

00:10:10.189 --> 00:10:11.189
we have.

00:10:11.189 --> 00:10:12.639
That's what we're here for.

00:10:12.639 --> 00:10:16.689
That's a world where stability and peace come
from making everyone certain about exactly

00:10:16.689 --> 00:10:20.810
when and how the US will use military force.

00:10:20.810 --> 00:10:25.300
But there's no such thing as perfect certainty,
especially when the technology and norms of

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:28.170
war are changing so quickly.

00:10:28.170 --> 00:10:32.850
America's challenge will be to constantly
create new norms, and clarify new lines of

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:34.230
deterrence, to keep pace with those changes.

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:35.160
It's a different role than we're used to,
but one that we'll need to learn quickly.

